# Design Problem Framework

A mental model for class/object design questions (LRU Cache, MinStack, Insert
Delete GetRandom, etc.). These sit one level above normal LeetCode: you combine
data structures to hit a complexity target on every operation.

## The three questions to start with
1. What are the operations, and what complexity does each one require?
2. What is the best conceivable runtime (for each operation)?
3. What data structure lets me do this or that in that time?

## The key difference from normal LeetCode
In a normal problem, the best conceivable runtime is ONE thing for the whole
problem ("I have to scan the array at least once, so O(n)"). In design, EACH
operation has its own requirement, and sometimes they compete. That tension is
the difficulty and the point. No single structure usually satisfies all
operations, so you combine several.

## Structure brainstorming (keep this map in your head)
When you ask "what lets me do X in O(something)?", recall what each gives:

```
hash map:            lookup / insert / delete by key in O(1)
                     but NO order

array / vector:      index access O(1)
                     but insert/delete in the middle O(n)

doubly linked list:  insert/delete at any point O(1) IF you hold the pointer
                     but search O(n)

heap:                min/max in O(1)
                     insert/pop in O(log n)

balanced BST / set:  search, insert, delete, and order in O(log n)
                     min/max in O(log n)
```

When one operation needs something a structure does not give, find another that
does, and combine them.

> "What structure gives me this operation in the required time? If no single one
> gives them all, which combination does, and how do I connect them?"

## The full process
1. List the operations and each one's required complexity.
2. Best conceivable runtime per operation.
3. Attack each operation separately: what structure makes it efficient?
4. For the hard operation, reason why the obvious structures fail
   (heap is O(log n), array delete-in-middle is O(n), etc.).
5. Find the structure that meets the exact requirement.
6. Connect the structures: how does one point into the other?
7. Sync them: what info does each structure need to update the other?

---

## Worked example: LRU Cache

Operations: get O(1), put O(1). Evict least-recently-used when full.

1. Two operations, both O(1). The hard part is tracking use order in O(1).
2. Lookup by key O(1) -> hash map. Order tracking O(1) -> ?
3. A min-heap of timestamps was tempting but is O(log n) to reorder. Rejected.
4. Need: move a used item to "most recent" and drop the "least recent", both
   O(1). Removing from the middle in O(1) needs access to neighbors ->
   doubly linked list.
5. Connect: hash map stores key -> pointer to the node in the list.
   get: find node via map (O(1)), move it in the list (O(1)).
6. Sync: the node stores its KEY (not just value) so that on eviction you can
   erase it from the map with cache.erase(node->key).

Combination: hash map (O(1) lookup) + doubly linked list (O(1) reorder).
Dummy first/last nodes remove the head/tail edge cases.

---

## Worked example: MinStack

Operations: push, pop, top, getMin, all O(1).

1. push/pop/top are trivially O(1) on a stack. The hard one is getMin O(1).
2. If you only store values, getMin means scanning: O(n). Need it precomputed.
3. The min changes on pop. If the current min leaves, you need the previous min.
   -> store the min at each level.
4. Structure: a stack of pairs {value, min-so-far}, or a parallel min-stack.
5. Connect: each element carries the min of everything below it.
6. getMin just reads the top's stored min. O(1).

Combination: one stack, each entry augmented with precomputed min state.

---

## The recurring meta-pattern
Decompose by operation -> pick a structure per operation -> combine when no
single one suffices -> connect them (one holds pointers/indices into the other)
-> keep them in sync (each side stores what it needs to update the other).

Trade space for time: O(1) queries on dynamic data almost always come from
maintaining precomputed auxiliary state that you update on every modification.
